<span class="kicker">2026-08-10 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Install a Paver Front Walkway</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>A front walkway does more work than any other path on a property. It’s the first thing guests, delivery drivers, and anyone passing by actually experiences of your home, and it sets a tone before a single knock on the door. That makes design decisions here carry more weight than they would for a side path or garden trail tucked out of view. Getting the installation right matters just as much as it does anywhere else, but getting the design right is where a front walkway earns its keep.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, front walkways are some of the most requested projects we take on across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, precisely because homeowners understand how much curb appeal rides on this one feature. This guide covers both the design decisions that make a front walkway feel intentional and welcoming, and the full installation process to bring that design to life.</p>
<h2><b>Why Front Walkway Design Deserves Extra Thought</b></h2>
<p>Unlike a backyard path that mostly needs to function well, a front walkway is on display constantly, viewed from the street, from a car pulling into the driveway, and up close by anyone approaching the door. It’s also one of the more affordable ways to meaningfully improve a home’s curb appeal, since it doesn’t require the scale of investment a full landscape renovation does, while still having an outsized visual impact on how the whole front of the house reads.</p>
<h2><b>Designing a Front Walkway That Complements Your Home</b></h2>
<h3><b>Matching the Walkway to Your Home’s Architectural Style</b></h3>
<p>A front walkway should generally echo the style of the home it leads to, rather than fighting against it. A traditional brick colonial pairs naturally with brick or classic running bond concrete pavers, while a more modern home with clean architectural lines often suits a simpler stack bond pattern in a larger format paver. A mountain or craftsman style home, common across parts of the Denver area, tends to pair well with natural stone or a tumbled, textured concrete paver that echoes a more organic, rustic feel.</p>
<h3><b>Width and Proportion</b></h3>
<p>A front entry walkway is typically the widest walkway on a property, generally 4 to 5 feet, wide enough for two people to walk comfortably side by side. Beyond function, this width also needs to feel proportional to the scale of the home’s entry, a narrow walkway leading to a substantial front door and porch can look visually undersized, while a walkway sized well against that entry reads as intentional and welcoming. Our guide on<a href="/blog/what-is-the-standard-width-of-a-paver-walkway/"> standard paver walkway width</a> breaks down width considerations by use case in more depth.</p>
<h3><b>Symmetry and Approach Line</b></h3>
<p>Many front walkways work best running in a straight, direct, and symmetrical line from the driveway or street to the front door, particularly on homes with a centered entry. That said, a gentle curve can add visual interest and feels appropriate on properties with more informal landscaping or a less centered entry point. Our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-make-a-curved-paver-walkway/"> how to make a curved paver walkway</a> covers what goes into building a curve well if that’s the direction you’re leaning.</p>
<h3><b>Lighting Along the Path</b></h3>
<p>Low-voltage path lighting along a front walkway serves both function and design. It improves visibility and safety for guests arriving after dark, and it adds a warm, finished look to the front of a home in the evening that a walkway without lighting simply doesn’t have. Fixtures are typically spaced every 6 to 8 feet along the path, positioned to illuminate the walking surface without creating glare toward the house or street.</p>
<h3><b>Plantings and Border Treatment</b></h3>
<p>The plants and border treatment alongside a front walkway do a lot to soften the hardscape and tie it into the rest of the yard. Low, structured plantings, like boxwood or ornamental grasses, work well for a more formal look, while a looser mix of perennials and native plants suits a more relaxed, naturalistic front yard. Leaving a narrow planting bed on either side of the walkway, rather than running pavers edge to edge with turf, generally reads as more finished and gives you room for that kind of softening.</p>
<h2><b>Checking HOA Guidelines Before You Start</b></h2>
<p>If your property is part of a homeowners association, it’s worth checking your HOA’s guidelines before finalizing your front walkway design, since front-facing changes are more likely to fall under review than backyard projects that aren’t visible from the street. Some HOAs regulate paver color, material type, or even walkway width for front-facing installations, and confirming this upfront avoids a design you love running into an approval problem partway through planning. This varies significantly between neighborhoods across Denver, Broomfield, and Parker, so a quick check with your specific HOA is worth the time before ordering materials.</p>
<h2><b>What You’ll Need</b></h2>
<p><b>Materials</b></p>
<ul><li>Pavers in your chosen style, plus 5 to 10 percent extra for cuts and waste</li><li>Crushed gravel or road base, 4 to 6 inches deep depending on soil type</li><li>Landscape fabric</li><li>Coarse bedding sand</li><li>Polymeric sand for joints</li><li>Edge restraints and spikes</li><li>Low-voltage lighting fixtures and wiring, if including path lighting</li></ul>
<p><b>Tools</b></p>
<ul><li>Shovel and spade</li><li>Rubber mallet</li><li>String line and stakes</li><li>4 foot level</li><li>Paver saw or angle grinder with a masonry blade</li><li>Push broom</li></ul>

